>
    <p class="lede">
        Canada, he said, is known for being first to be second. He does not
        yet believe it will choose to lead.
    </p>
    <p>
        He is co-founder and CEO of TransPod, a ten-year-old deep-tech company
        working on high-speed transportation. People know the idea as
        Hyperloop. In South Korea it is called Hyper Tube. The same work is
        underway in South Korea, China, and Canada.
    </p>
    <p>
        A train cannot go faster than 300 kilometres an hour, because of
        aerodynamic friction. The old answer, he said, is to run the vehicle
        in a tube with most of the air removed, so it can keep accelerating.
        On the ground that is a steel tube on concrete pillars. The vehicle
        looks like an aircraft without wings, held by a magnetic system, at
        about the speed of an airplane. He called it something we already
        know how to do. His horizon for defining the transportation system of
        this century is before 2028, the way the railway defined an earlier
        Canada.
    </p>
    <p>
        TransPod has worked with the Alberta government since 2020. The
        province's Passenger Rail Master Plan is about connecting Edmonton and
        Calgary. The plan had been to take three years before a request for
        proposals. Premier Danielle Smith, the Monday before the kickoff,
        moved that request forward, from an expression of interest to a
        proposal before the end of the year, with a preferred bidder in April
        2027. Within six months, TransPod could be chosen to connect the two
        cities.
    </p>
    <p>
        He is skeptical. A country he hears described every day as first to be
        second would have to wake up and decide it wants to lead
        twenty-first-century transportation. He asked whether that would be
        because of the Trump administration, because Canada is struggling
        financially, or because it wants a new industry. He tied it to Greg
        Hart's point: old thinking drives old habits, and then failure.
    </p>
    <p>Two refusals, and one that arrives only after the money is no longer needed.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Under the Trudeau government, TransPod applied twice to the
            Strategic Innovation Fund and was denied twice. The first answer:
            the fit was good, but the fund did not understand the intellectual
            property, so it would not invest. The second was a letter that
            said thank you, but no, and gave no reason.
        </li>
        <li>
            Invest Ontario told him the company checked the boxes, and then
            that it would not invest until TransPod closed a $400 million
            round. His question back: you will invest when we no longer need
            the money? The answer he heard was yes.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        He said that is the feedback across the board, and that this is the
        culture that has to change if Canada wants to lead by 2028 and stand
        on the world stage.
    </p>
    <p>
        Export Development Canada told him things are changing, and offered
        the picture he liked: Canada is a bear coming out of hibernation. The
        test, next spring, is whether the bear stays awake. He takes Premier
        Smith's earlier request for proposals as a good signal. He does not
        yet know if it is a decision to choose tomorrow's technology, or a
        return to a conventional train that does not pay for itself.
    </p>
